## Premise
Spelling differences are not very obvious, or at least, worth noticing to most people. But these, inadvertently or not, split English dialects apart. 

There are already tons of English dialects: British, American, Canadian, Indian, Australian, and many other places where English holds an official status. But there is a massive rift between two primary sides, the British (and the rest of Commonwealth) and American. Namely so, but they are only a product of standardization, based on a dictionary, written by possibly personal preferences, like in the case of Webster's Dictionary, which later was dubbed "a spelling reform". After all, any reform is just a shift in preferences. 

However, let's not be into judging the history. I simply just have an idea: what if there was truly an international standard in English usage and spelling, or, more appopriately, would there be a spelling guideline that helps bridge the world's various kinds of English?

Still, this would be another personal preference. But that would be the preference I see fit, and hope people can adopt it if they ever desire.



## The idea

Let's take the *Oxford English Dictionary (OED)* as a baseline. It is based on British standards, but with exception of -ize affix to better reflect the Latin root of a vast swathe of lexicons.

 Personally, this is what I am advocating; in fact, just a few months earlier, you can notice me writing words with -ise affix. I only recently changed my spelling because, once again, of my preference, but it is also my commit of learning English, and the desire to deeply explore this language. I am not a native speaker by any mean, and I'm actively using and learning it everyday.

After getting around based on the OED, I realize that English can be more diversified by allowing some dialect-specific features beyond being purely British. So I came up with a set of modifications for the Oxford spelling.